    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Athletes with poor psychological wellbeing (i.e., low self-esteem, high anxiety and depression) are more susceptible to negative teammates influences (i.e., modelling of teammates' disordered eating, perceived pressure from teammates to lose weight), while athletes with better psychological wellbeing (i.e., high self-esteem, low anxiety and depression) are protected against negative teammate influences and may reap the benefits of having supportive teammate friendships. Furthermore, the mediating effect of psychological wellbeing is similar for both male and female athletes."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "Although age and BMI were controlled for, it is acknowledged that there are a number of additional co-variates (e.g., perfectionism; Madigan, Stoeber, & Passfield, 2017; athletic identity; Turton, Goodwin, & Meyer, 2017) that could have contributed to the findings of this study. In addition, while a three wave prospective design increases confidence in the temporal precedence of variables, the true causal role of teammate influences can only be definitively established using experimental research designs."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Future research should consider employing experimental designs to determine the true causal role of teammates on athletes' actual eating and exercise behaviours. Building on the mediation findings presented, to move the field forward additional research is now required employing modelling analytic techniques to further our understanding of the complex, multi-factorial influences on athlete eating and exercise behaviours, which in turn could help to inform further theoretical developments."
    },
